| # Where a launch has to go to a shell, GNU Make assembles `$(SHELL)
| # $(.SHELLFLAGS) LINE` as text and copies the SHELL in one character at a time,
| # putting a backslash in front of every character special to the shell (job.c).
| # Its comment gives the reason it was written for — without the escaping the
| # assembly recurses "ad nauseam, or until stack overflow" — and the reason it
| # matters from a Makefile is this: a `SHELL` holding a command substitution is
| # a NAME with those characters in it, not a command to run.
| #
| # GNU Make 4.4.1 escapes the `$` and the `(`, so its own tokenizer hands the
| # line back as the words `./sh$(touch`, `pwned)`, `-c`, `echo one > out` and
| # execs the first as a path. It is not there, the build stops at 127, and
| # neither `pwned` nor `out` is ever written.
| #
| # THE GATE IS THE FILE. `pwned` is what the substitution would leave behind, and
| # the recording holds a build in which it does not exist.
| SHELL := ./sh$$(touch pwned)
| all:
| 	@echo one > out
